# busybox options PlexOS needs and Buildroot's default configuration turns off.
#
# Applied on top of package/busybox/busybox.config via
# BR2_PACKAGE_BUSYBOX_CONFIG_FRAGMENT_FILES. Every option here must be checked against
# the generated .config after a build: kconfig drops options with unmet dependencies
# without erroring, and this project has already lost a toolchain to that.

# Without this, `tar` cannot read a .xz archive at all -- not with -J, not by sniffing
# the stream. That is the whole of ADR-0010's provisioning path: a Debian package is an
# ar archive of control.tar.xz and data.tar.xz, and plexos-plex shells out to tar for
# both. Provisioning got as far as verifying Plex's GPG signature and matching every
# member against the signed manifest before failing to read the version out of
# control.tar.xz, on an appliance whose kernel has CONFIG_XZ_DEC compiled in.
#
# busybox defaults this to y and has no dependencies on it; Buildroot's own busybox.config
# is what turns it off. Nothing else in the image provides a tar, so this is the fix
# rather than one of several.
#
# xz only, deliberately. A .gz member would not be found in the first place --
# plexos_plex looks for members named control.tar.xz and data.tar.xz -- so enabling
# FEATURE_SEAMLESS_GZ would buy nothing and would look like support that is not there.
# ADR-0010 treats a change in Plex's packaging as an event to notice, not to absorb.
CONFIG_FEATURE_SEAMLESS_XZ=y

# busybox's losetup has no --show, so it cannot say which device it attached, and
# plexos_plex::execute::attach_loop needs the answer to mount the app image. util-linux's
# losetup does have it and is already in the image. Turning busybox's off means exactly
# one program is /sbin/losetup, rather than whichever package Buildroot installed last.
# CONFIG_LOSETUP is not set
